content.text:
**No — keep them separate.** They look the same, but they mean different things.

| | `RegistrySource` | `GitHubRegistry` |
|---|---|---|
| Role | Parsed *address* (“where?”) | *Client* that fetches (“how?”) |
| Crate | `agm-core` (no HTTP) | `agm-registry` (octocrab, I/O) |
| Shape | Enum — room for more backends | Struct + `impl Registry` |

Python analogy: `RegistrySource` is like a frozen dataclass / typed URL; `GitHubRegistry` is like a session/client that uses that address. You wouldn’t fold `httpx.Client` into the URL string type.

`registry_for` is the intentional bridge:

```7:10:crates/agm-registry/src/lib.rs
pub fn registry_for(source: RegistrySource) -> impl Registry {
    match source {
        RegistrySource::GitHub { owner, repo } => GitHubRegistry { owner, repo },
    }
}
```

Merging them would either drag network code into `agm-core`, or pull parsing into the I/O crate — both worse than a few duplicated fields.

If the twin fields bother you, the lazy fix is a one-liner `From`, not one type:

```rust
impl From<RegistrySource> for GitHubRegistry {
    fn from(source: RegistrySource) -> Self {
        match source {
            RegistrySource::GitHub { owner, repo } => Self { owner, repo },
        }
    }
}
```

Or store `source: RegistrySource` inside `GitHubRegistry` and match in methods — only worth it when you add a second `RegistrySource` variant and the match grows.

